#### `StreamTextResult.fullStream` Renamed to `stream`

The full event stream returned by `streamText` has been renamed from
`fullStream` to `stream`.

```tsx title="AI SDK 6"
const result = streamText({
  model: yourModel,
  prompt: 'Hello!',
});

for await (const part of result.fullStream) {
  console.log(part);
}
```

```tsx title="AI SDK 7"
const result = streamText({
  model: yourModel,
  prompt: 'Hello!',
});

for await (const part of result.stream) {
  console.log(part);
}
```


The `fullStream` property is still available as a deprecated alias for
`stream`.

#### `streamText` `onChunk` Receives All Stream Parts

In AI SDK 7, `streamText` calls `onChunk` for every `TextStreamPart` emitted by
the stream. In AI SDK 6, `onChunk` only received a subset of stream parts such as
text deltas, reasoning deltas, sources, tool calls, tool input deltas, tool
results, custom parts, and raw chunks.

Update handlers that assume the old subset to guard the chunk types they process:

```tsx title="AI SDK 7"
const result = streamText({
  model: yourModel,
  prompt: 'Hello',
  onChunk({ chunk }) {
    if (chunk.type === 'text-delta') {
      console.log(chunk.text);
    }
  },
});
```


`onChunk` can now also receive lifecycle, boundary, and terminal parts such as
`start`, `start-step`, `text-start`, `text-end`, `reasoning-start`,
`reasoning-end`, `tool-input-end`, `finish-step`, `finish`, `abort`, and
`error`.

#### Move `includeRawChunks` to `include.rawChunks`

The top-level `includeRawChunks` option on `streamText` is deprecated in AI SDK 7. Move it into the `include` options object as `rawChunks`.

```tsx title="AI SDK 6"
const result = streamText({
  model: yourModel,
  prompt: 'Hello',
  includeRawChunks: true,
});
```

```tsx title="AI SDK 7"
const result = streamText({
  model: yourModel,
  prompt: 'Hello',
  include: {
    rawChunks: true,
  },
});
```


The deprecated top-level option continues to work in AI SDK 7, so you can migrate incrementally.

#### Rename `experimental_include` to `include`

The `experimental_include` option for `generateText`, `streamText`, and
`ToolLoopAgent` is now stable and has been renamed to `include`.

```tsx title="AI SDK 6"
const result = await generateText({
  model: yourModel,
  prompt: 'Hello',
  experimental_include: {
    requestBody: false,
  },
});
```

```tsx title="AI SDK 7"
const result = await generateText({
  model: yourModel,
  prompt: 'Hello',
  include: {
    requestBody: false,
  },
});
```


The deprecated `experimental_include` name continues to work for backwards
compatibility. If both `include` and `experimental_include` are provided,
`include` takes precedence.

#### Request and Response Bodies Are Excluded by Default

In AI SDK 7, `generateText` and `streamText` no longer include request bodies in
step results by default. `generateText` also no longer includes response bodies
by default. This reduces memory usage, especially when prompts or provider
responses contain large payloads such as images or files.

If your application reads `result.request.body`, `step.request.body`,
`result.response.body`, or `step.response.body`, opt in with `include`.

Some providers such as [Perplexity](/providers/ai-sdk-providers/perplexity#sources) and
[Google](/providers/ai-sdk-providers/google#sources) include sources in the response.

Sources can reference URLs or documents. You can access them using the `sources`
property of the result. For OpenAI Responses and Anthropic web search, sources
contain the retrieved references, which can include more references than the
model cites in its answer. OpenAI file-search results are also exposed as document
sources when the API returns those results. If retrieval data is unavailable,
citation-derived references remain available as sources for compatibility.

Each `url` source contains the following properties:

- `id`: The ID of the source.
- `url`: The URL of the source.
- `title`: The optional title of the source.
- `providerMetadata`: Provider metadata for the source.

When you use `generateText`, you can access the sources using the `sources` property:

```ts
const result = await generateText({
  model: 'google/gemini-3.8-flash',
  tools: {
    google_search: google.tools.googleSearch({}),
  },
  prompt: 'List the top 5 San Francisco news from the past week.',
});

for (const source of result.sources) {
  if (source.sourceType === 'url') {
    console.log('ID:', source.id);
    console.log('Title:', source.title);
    console.log('URL:', source.url);
    console.log('Provider metadata:', source.providerMetadata);
    console.log();
  }
}
```

When you use `streamText`, you can access the sources using the `stream` property:

```tsx
const result = streamText({
  model: 'google/gemini-3.8-flash',
  tools: {
    google_search: google.tools.googleSearch({}),
  },
  prompt: 'List the top 5 San Francisco news from the past week.',
});

for await (const part of result.stream) {
  if (part.type === 'source' && part.sourceType === 'url') {
    console.log('ID:', part.id);
    console.log('Title:', part.title);
    console.log('URL:', part.url);
    console.log('Provider metadata:', part.providerMetadata);
    console.log();
  }
}
```

The sources are also available in the `result.sources` promise.

Streaming speech is an experimental feature.

Use `experimental_streamSpeech` when you want audio before the full text is
synthesized, or when the text itself arrives incrementally (for example from
`streamText`). The function uses speech models with streaming support.

```ts
import { elevenLabs } from '@ai-sdk/elevenlabs';
import { experimental_streamSpeech as streamSpeech, streamText } from 'ai';

const { textStream } = streamText({
  model: 'openai/gpt-5-mini',
  prompt: 'Tell me a short story.',
});

const result = streamSpeech({
  model: elevenLabs.speech('eleven_flash_v2_5'),
  text: textStream, // a string, or an array or stream of strings and/or text parts
  voice: '21m00Tcm4TlvDq8ikWAM',
  outputFormat: { type: 'audio/mpeg' },
});

for await (const chunk of result.audioStream) {
  // chunk is a Uint8Array of audio bytes in generation order
}

console.log(await result.usage); // e.g. { inputCharacters: 512 }
```

`text` accepts a complete string or a stream of text deltas. Providers with an
incremental text transport synthesize deltas as they arrive; others read the
text stream to completion first. The end of the text stream ends the input.

`text` can also contain text parts, `{ type: 'text', text, voice?, providerOptions? }`,
to give parts of the text their own voice (for example for dialogue) on providers
that support several voices per stream. Single-voice providers error the stream
when a part's `voice` differs from the call's `voice`. A script that is known up
front can be passed as an array:

```ts
const result = streamSpeech({
  model,
  text: [
    { type: 'text', text: 'Did you hear? ', voice: alice },
    { type: 'text', text: 'No, what?', voice: bob },
  ],
});
```

`error` parts from the provider are not fatal: `fullStream` includes them,
`audioStream` skips them, and each one is also passed to `onError` (which
defaults to `console.error`).

Use `fullStream` instead of `audioStream` when you need per-chunk metadata:
each `audio` part carries the chunk `audioFormat` (e.g.
`{ type: 'audio/pcm', rate: 24000 }`) and optional `providerMetadata`. Audio
chunks are not standalone files; concatenate them in order.

`audioStream` and `fullStream` share one live stream, so only one of them can
be accessed, once. Access the stream before awaiting `usage`, `warnings`,
`responses`, or `providerMetadata`; accessing a result promise first consumes
the stream internally.

